<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French National Committee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Comité national français</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French National Committee was the governing body of Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in September 1941. Its members held portfolios like ministers, and it gained growing recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> as the representative of fighting France. In June 1943 it merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a>'s command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-national-committee.jpg" alt="French National Committee" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (Keystone-France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comit%C3%A9_national_fran%C3%A7ais.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The committee succeeded the smaller Empire Defense Council. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> who suggested that de Gaulle form a committee, to give his leadership an appearance of more constitutional and less dictatorial authority. According to the historian Henri Bernard, de Gaulle accepted the proposal but took care to leave out his adversaries within the Free France movement, such as Émile Muselier and André Labarthe, keeping only men who would agree with him. The committee was founded on 24 September 1941 by an edict de Gaulle signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The committee had six civilian and six military members, each a commissioner for a field of government, with de Gaulle as president. René Pleven held economy, finance and the colonies and coordinated the civilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>; Paul Legentilhomme was commissioner of war; Maurice Dejean held foreign affairs until his dismissal on 18 October 1942, after which the post passed to Pleven for a time and then to René Massigli. René Cassin took justice and public instruction, André Diethelm action in the Metropolis, work and information, and André Philip the interior.</p><p>Martial Valin was commissioner of the air force. Muselier held the navy and merchant navy until he resigned on 3 March 1942 and was replaced by Rear Admiral Philippe Auboyneau. From 4 March 1942 Georges Catroux and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-thierry-dargenlieu">Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu</a> served as commissioners at large, and Jacques Soustelle became commissioner of information on 27 July 1942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 20 March 1943 the committee secretly appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-moulin">Jean Moulin</a>, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as its representative in Metropolitan France and national commissioner on active duty, charged with creating a single coordinating body for the French Resistance. In the view of Daniel Cordier, Moulin was then becoming one of the main figures of the internal Resistance. The committee ended on 3 June 1943, when it merged with the French Civil and Military High Command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French National Committee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>French National Committee</b> was set up in London in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-france">Free France</a></li></ul></section>
